import{_ as t,o as n,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.C3idr2hZ.js";const a="/orc/screens/notification-center-light-1440.png",i="/orc/screens/mobile-notifications-dark-390.png",r="/orc/screens/mobile-notifications-project-390.png",d="/orc/screens/mobile-notifications-group-390.png",y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Notifications","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/notifications.md","filePath":"docs/notifications.md","lastUpdated":1794413839000}'),c={name:"docs/notifications.md"};function l(p,e,u,h,g,m){return n(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="notifications" tabindex="-1">Notifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#notifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Notifications&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The bell in the workspace header opens your notification inbox: an anchored menu on desktop and a full page at <code>/notifications</code> on phones, including while the chat is open. New arrivals show an unread indicator without opening it. Use <strong>Alt+N</strong> or <strong>Open notifications</strong> in the command palette to open it; on phones both navigate to the page, and its back bar returns to where you were.</p><p>Notifications cover human questions, approvals and commands, agent questions and outcomes, and job outcomes. Open an item to review the request or visit its source. Request forms stay collapsed until opened; drafts survive closing and reopening the menu. Read and dismissed state is stored on the server and shared by clients. Dismissal does not approve, answer, cancel or delete a source request.</p><p>The inbox shows <strong>unread notifications</strong> by default. Opening an item or choosing <strong>Mark read</strong> clears it from the inbox. Clicking a native desktop notification opens its destination and marks that item read; a grouped done toast only opens its destination. <strong>Show read</strong> includes read history; use <strong>Mark unread</strong> to return an item to the inbox, or <strong>Mark all read</strong> to clear it. An opened human request stays available while you answer it.</p><p>Desktop and phone share one inbox logic. Updates from the same session or request collapse into a quiet summary row with a count and the most urgent state; expand it to see each update. <strong>Latest</strong> lists groups newest first; <strong>By project</strong> adds a section per project with sticky headers and counts, and the choice persists across reloads. <strong>Needs you</strong> narrows the inbox to items that require an action. Human requests keep their review card in the row, so questions, approvals and commands are answered inline without leaving the inbox; drafts survive closing and reopening. Phone rows keep mark-read and dismiss in one named row menu with 44px targets; desktop rows keep direct icons.</p><p>Each session keeps only its latest <strong>Agent finished</strong> notification. A newer finish physically replaces older completion notifications, including read and dismissed ones. Existing duplicates are cleaned automatically in bounded batches; lists and unread counts show only the latest even while cleanup is catching up. Saved chat views and the native session share that latest completion using the agent backend, execution host and native session ID. The alert opens where the latest turn ran; opening another read-only view creates no new completion alert. Matching native IDs on different hosts or agent backends stay separate. When a session continues, its latest completion alert is automatically marked read. This applies to workspace chats and observed agent sessions. <strong>Show read</strong> includes the retained latest completion, rather than a history of every turn. Failures and pending questions or approvals are kept independently.</p><p><img src="'+a+'" alt="Read history on desktop with demo data"></p><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="The phone inbox as a full page with demo data"></p><p><img src="'+r+'" alt="Phone inbox grouped by project with demo data"></p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt="A session&#39;s updates collapsed into one summary row, expanded"></p><p>Job delivery follows <code>notify_on</code>: <code>never</code> creates no alert, <code>failure</code> reports failed runs, and <code>always</code> also reports successful runs. Notification types are extensible strings; unknown types use the same common menu and source link.</p><h2 id="expiry-and-audit-history" tabindex="-1">Expiry and audit history <a class="header-anchor" href="#expiry-and-audit-history" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Expiry and audit history&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every notification has an expiry date. <strong>Settings → Storage → Automatic cleanup</strong> sets notification retention (default <strong>30 days</strong>) and audit retention (default <strong>90 days</strong>), each from 1 to 3650 days. These are server-wide preferences. All notifications expire, including unresolved ones. The underlying request has its own timeout and lifecycle; notification expiry never resolves it.</p><p>Expired entries disappear from reads immediately. The API server cleans stored rows at startup and every minute in bounded batches of 1000. A backlog drains over successive passes. Lowering retention also hides older entries immediately. SQLite reuses freed pages; cleanup does not guarantee a smaller database file.</p><p><strong>Activity → Audit</strong> retains notification creation, resolution, read, dismissal, expiry and supersession events independently, including the actual notification title and message. Audit events expire under their own retention period even if the alert has already been cleaned up. New lifecycle events also cover human-request status changes, job starts/outcomes and task creation/status transitions. They exclude unrelated source prompts, command payloads, responses and output. Repeated polling and unchanged state do not create audit events. Existing audit snapshots keep their original content until their retention period ends.</p><h2 id="sound-push-and-channels" tabindex="-1">Sound, push and channels <a class="header-anchor" href="#sound-push-and-channels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sound, push and channels&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Desktop browsers show settled toasts; Needs-you alerts can chime and Done alerts are silent. <strong>Settings → Notifications → Sound</strong> controls browser-local sound and project muting. Browser/PWA push remains separately opt-in for background human requests, chat replies and job results; see <a href="./install">Installation</a>. A device receives job pushes only after <strong>Job results</strong> is enabled there. Existing devices subscribed to every topic receive this new topic on upgrade; you can turn it off independently, and that choice survives restarts. Enabling it does not replay older runs. Each alert follows <code>notify_on</code> and includes the job name, exit status, duration and a link to the run. <code>failure</code> sends failed runs, <code>always</code> also sends successful runs, and <code>never</code> sends nothing.</p><h3 id="forward-notifications-to-telegram-or-slack" tabindex="-1">Forward notifications to Telegram or Slack <a class="header-anchor" href="#forward-notifications-to-telegram-or-slack" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Forward notifications to Telegram or Slack&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In <strong>Settings → Channels → Notification forwarding</strong>, enter the ORC origin reachable from your channel client (for example <code>https://orc.example.com</code>), enable forwarding and save. The URL must have no credentials, path, query or fragment. Links open the existing authenticated ORC app; forwarding supplies no login or approval authority. The forwarding switch and URL stay disabled while saved settings load or save. If loading fails, the form shows the error and stays disabled.</p><p>Edit a connection, open <strong>Recipients</strong>, edit a recipient and select <strong>Forward notifications</strong>. Apply the recipient and save the connection. The connection and <strong>Allow outbound</strong> must be enabled. Inbound messaging and sender IDs are unnecessary for these outbound-only alerts. Existing recipient project restrictions apply; restricted recipients never receive unassigned or other-project notifications.</p><p><strong>Notification filters and preview</strong> selects types, categories (<code>human</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>job</code>), severity (<code>needs-you</code>, <code>done</code>) and outcome (<code>waiting</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>). Values within a group are alternatives; selected groups must all match. Empty groups match all known values. Unknown types are excluded unless <strong>Include new or unknown types</strong> is selected; they have no known outcome and cannot match an outcome filter. <strong>Preview filters</strong> evaluates the draft without sending a message.</p><p>Forwarding sends fixed, escaped summaries and source links. It excludes titles, question text, command arguments, tool payloads, logs and credentials. Job events honor <code>notify_on</code>; human requests also retain their producer&#39;s <code>notify</code> provider selection. Selecting forwarding replaces the recipient&#39;s legacy <strong>Interactive questions and approvals</strong> delivery with safe links, avoiding duplicate request messages. Interactive delivery retains its own inbound/sender permissions.</p><p>Clear a recipient&#39;s <strong>Forward notifications</strong>, or pause globally, without disconnecting the channel or changing in-app alerts. Activation or changes to a recipient&#39;s filters, destination or project restrictions start with new events; history and events produced during a global pause are not replayed. An in-flight send can finish after a pause. Saved configuration survives restart.</p><p><strong>Recent deliveries</strong> shows status, attempt count, safe errors and a channel receipt ID through the API. Stable notification/destination IDs prevent replay of completed deliveries after restart. Definite provider rate limits retry at most four times with bounded exponential backoff (4, 8, 16 seconds). Ambiguous network/timeouts and interrupted sends are marked <code>unknown</code> for inspection, rather than automatically resent. Permanent errors are <code>failed</code>. Expired events are never newly dispatched; errors stay in delivery history/logs and do not create recursive notifications.</p><p>The authenticated API exposes <code>GET/PUT /api/channels/notification-policy</code>, <code>POST /api/channels/notification-preview</code> and <code>GET /api/channels/notification-deliveries?limit=50</code> (maximum 100). The preview body is <code>{filter, project_ids, event: {type, level, project_id}}</code>. These use the existing <code>channel_send</code> transport, destinations and secure credentials.</p><p>Links to <code>/activity?notifications=open</code> (optionally with <code>&amp;request=&lt;id&gt;</code>) open the notification menu on desktop and redirect to the phone page, which expands the linked request. <code>/notifications</code> is shareable at every viewport size. See <a href="./human-requests">Human requests</a> for source request actions and timeouts, and <a href="./api">API reference</a> for notification and audit routes.</p>',26)])])}const v=t(c,[["render",l]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
